# Continuous Development, CI & CD

## Jenkins — Build Orchestration Tool

Jenkins is used to automate and orchestrate CI/CD activities.

For a Java application, Jenkins can automate:

```text
Clone Source Code
       |
       v
Compile
       |
       v
Unit Testing
       |
       v
Code Coverage
       |
       v
Build Artifact
       |
       v
Archive / Publish Artifact
       |
       v
Deploy
```

---

# Application Build

A **Build** is the process of compiling source code and creating a deployable artifact.

Example:

```text
Java Source Code
       |
       v
Java Compiler
       |
       v
Compiled Code
       |
       v
WAR / JAR
```

Java compiler:

```bash
javac
```

Common build tools:

```text
Ant
Maven
Gradle
```

---

# Maven Build Tool

Maven is a Java build and project-management tool used to:

* Compile Java source code
* Execute unit tests
* Manage dependencies
* Execute plugins
* Package applications
* Create artifacts
* Perform build-related activities

Common Maven command:

```bash
mvn clean package
```

---

# Maven Project Structure

Typical Java Maven project:

```text
Java_Web_Application_Project/
|
├── src/
│   ├── main/
│   │   ├── java/
│   │   │   ├── Application.java
│   │   │   ├── Controller.java
│   │   │   └── Service.java
│   │   │
│   │   └── resources/
│   │       ├── application.properties
│   │       ├── *.html
│   │       ├── *.css
│   │       └── *.js
│   │
│   └── test/
│       └── *.java
│
├── target/
│   └── application.war / application.jar
│
└── pom.xml
```

### Important directories

**src/main**

Contains application source code and resources.

**src/test**

Contains unit tests and test-related code.

**target**

Contains Maven build output and generated artifacts.

**pom.xml**

Main Maven project configuration file.

---

# pom.xml — Project Object Model

`pom.xml` stands for:

```text
Project Object Model
```

It is normally located in the root directory of a Maven project.

It defines:

* Project information
* Group ID
* Artifact ID
* Version
* Packaging
* Dependencies
* Plugins
* Build configuration
* Properties

Example:

```xml
<project>

    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>java-webapp</artifactId>
    <version>1.0</version>
    <packaging>war</packaging>

    <dependencies>
        ...
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            ...
        </plugins>
    </build>

</project>
```

---

# Maven Dependencies

Dependencies are external libraries required by the application.

Examples:

```text
JUnit
Selenium
Spring
Other Java Libraries
```

Dependencies are declared in `pom.xml`.

Example — JUnit:

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>junit</groupId>
    <artifactId>junit</artifactId>
    <version>4.13.2</version>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>
```

Important dependency fields:

```text
groupId
artifactId
version
scope
```

Maven repositories contain dependencies, plugins, and other artifacts.

Example public repository:

https://mvnrepository.com/

---

# Selenium Dependency

Selenium can be used for browser-based testing.

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.seleniumhq.selenium</groupId>
    <artifactId>selenium-java</artifactId>
    <version>4.35.0</version>
</dependency>
```

---

# Maven Plugins

Maven plugins provide additional functionality during the build.

Plugins can perform tasks such as:

```text
Compile
Test
Package
Code Coverage
Static Analysis
Deployment
```

Example:

```text
JaCoCo Maven Plugin
```

---

# Maven Repository

A Maven repository contains:

```text
Dependencies
Libraries
Plugins
Artifacts
```

During a build:

```text
pom.xml
   |
   v
Maven
   |
   v
Resolve Dependencies
   |
   v
Download Required Libraries
   |
   v
Compile / Test / Package
```

Maven stores downloaded dependencies in its local repository.

---

# Development Environment

Developers generally perform:

```text
1. Create Source Code
2. Compile Code
3. Run Unit Tests
4. Fix Issues
5. Commit Changes
6. Push Code to Git Repository
```

Common tools:

```text
IDE
JDK
Git
Maven
JUnit
Application Framework
```

Examples of IDEs:

```text
Eclipse
IntelliJ IDEA
Visual Studio Code
PyCharm
```

Source-code repositories can include:

```text
GitHub
GitLab
Bitbucket
Azure Repos
```

---

# Build Environment

The Build environment automates application building.

Typical process:

```text
Git Repository
      |
      v
Checkout Source Code
      |
      v
Read pom.xml
      |
      v
Resolve Dependencies
      |
      v
Compile
      |
      v
Unit Test
      |
      v
Code Coverage
      |
      v
Package
      |
      v
Create Artifact
      |
      v
Archive / Publish Artifact
```

---

# Test Environment

After a successful build, the application can be promoted to testing environments.

Examples:

```text
QA
UAT
```

Testing may include:

```text
Functional Testing
Integration Testing
Regression Testing
User Acceptance Testing
```

---

# Production Environment

After successful testing and approval, the application can be promoted to Production.

```text
Development
     |
     v
Build
     |
     v
QA
     |
     v
UAT
     |
     v
Production
```

---

# Source Code vs Artifacts

## Source Code

Source code is maintained in a source-code repository.

Example:

```text
Application.java v1.1
Application.java v1.2
Application.java v1.3
Application.java v1.4
```

Repository:

```text
GitHub
```

## Artifacts

Build artifacts are generated from source code.

Examples:

```text
application-1.1.war
application-1.2.war
application-1.3.war
application-1.4.war
```

Artifacts can be stored in:

```text
JFrog Artifactory
Azure Artifacts
Nexus Repository
```

Flow:

```text
Source Code
     |
     v
Git Repository
     |
     v
Maven Build
     |
     v
WAR / JAR
     |
     v
Artifact Repository
     |
     v
Deployment
```

---

# Application Build Process

### Input

```text
Source Code
```

Examples:

```text
*.java
pom.xml
Resources
Test Cases
```

### Output

```text
Build Artifact
```

Examples:

```text
*.war
*.jar
```

Overall:

```text
Source Code
     |
     | Maven
     v
Artifact
```

---

# Maven Goals and Commands

## Compile

```bash
mvn compile
```

Compiles the application source code.

## Test

```bash
mvn test
```

Runs the configured unit tests.

## Package

```bash
mvn package
```

Packages the application into its configured artifact format.

## Clean

```bash
mvn clean
```

Removes previous build output, normally the `target` directory.

## Clean and Package

```bash
mvn clean package
```

Cleans the previous build output and performs the package lifecycle.

## Install

```bash
mvn clean install
```

Builds the application and installs the resulting artifact into the local Maven repository.

---

# Maven Lifecycle

Simplified Maven lifecycle:

```text
validate
   |
   v
compile
   |
   v
test
   |
   v
package
   |
   v
verify
   |
   v
install
   |
   v
deploy
```

The actual behavior depends on the project's POM and configured plugins.

---

# WAR and JAR

For a Java web application:

```text
WAR
```

is commonly used for deployment to an application server.

For many standalone Java applications:

```text
JAR
```

is commonly used.

Examples:

```text
target/
├── application.war
```

or:

```text
target/
├── application.jar
```

---

# Unit Testing

Unit testing is the process of testing individual units/components of application code.

JUnit is a commonly used Java unit-testing framework.

Flow:

```text
Application Code
       |
       v
JUnit Test Cases
       |
       v
Pass / Fail
```

Maven command:

```bash
mvn test
```

---

# JaCoCo — Java Code Coverage

JaCoCo is a Java code-coverage tool.

It measures how much application code is exercised by automated tests.

Flow:

```text
Application Code
       |
       v
JUnit Tests
       |
       v
JaCoCo
       |
       v
Coverage Report
```

JaCoCo is normally configured as a **Maven plugin**.

Example:

```xml
<plugin>
    <groupId>org.jacoco</groupId>
    <artifactId>jacoco-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>0.8.13</version>

    <executions>

        <execution>
            <goals>
                <goal>prepare-agent</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>

        <execution>
            <id>report</id>
            <phase>test</phase>
            <goals>
                <goal>report</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>

    </executions>
</plugin>
```

Code coverage can include measurements such as:

```text
Line Coverage
Branch Coverage
Method Coverage
Class Coverage
```

Important:

```text
100% code coverage
```

does not necessarily mean that the application is defect-free. It indicates that the measured code was exercised by the tests.

